lign:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Global speech models struggle with Indic languages due to fundamental differences in prosodic rhythm. English is a stress-timed language where stressed syllables appear at roughly regular intervals, whereas Hindi and Dravidian languages are predominantly syllable-timed, with duration distributed more evenly across syllables. When an English-optimized architecture synthesizes Hindi, it imposes Western pitch contours and stress patterns, resulting in unnatural delivery.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Sarvam AI's Bulbul v3 addresses these challenges through dedicated regional acoustic modeling. It supports Hindi (hi-IN), Bengali (bn[span_169](start_span)[span_169](end_span)-IN), Tamil (ta-IN), Telugu (te-IN), Gujarati (gu-IN), Kannada (kn-IN), Malayalam (ml-IN), Marathi (mr-IN), Punjabi (pa-IN), Odia (od-IN), and Indian English (en-IN). The architecture incorporates specialized text normalization that automatically expands regional honorifics, dates, Indian Rupee currency formats, and ten-digit Indian phone groupings. Bulbul v3 processes code-mixed conversational Hinglish in a single forward pass, preventing the audio glitches and tone shifts that occur when pipelines chain separate language engines together.</span></p><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Deploying Bulbul v3 involves an important operational constraint: passing Romanized Indic text (e.g., "Aapka video ready hai") causes severe phonetic distortion. The model requires native Indic scripts (e.g., Devanagari: "à¤†à¤ªà¤•à¤¾ video ready à¤¹à¥ˆ"). The upstream orchestration pipeline must therefore route Romanized Indian text through an automated transliteration service prior to synthesis. Furthermore, Bulbul v3 does not support SSML tags; prosodic adjustments are limited to a coarse speed range of 0.5x to 2.0x.</span></p><h2 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Local / Open-Source TTS</span></h2><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Local open-source execution provides complete operational independence, eliminating external API usage costs, third-party network latency, and platform rate limits. However, compute requirements and licensing constraints vary significantly across models.</span></p><table style="border-spacing:0;border-collapse:collapse;margin-right:auto"><thead><tr style="height:0pt"><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" style="border-right-style:solid;border-bottom-color:#000000;border-top-width:1pt;border-right-width:1pt;border-left-color:#000000;vertical-align:top;border-right-color:#000000;border-left-width:1pt;border-top-style:solid;border-left-style:solid;border-bottom-width:1pt;width:66.9pt;border-top-color:#000000;border-bottom-style:solid"><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decorati