ht:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Conditional; requires mandatory public synthesis disclosure.</span></p></td></tr></tbody></table><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Kokoro-82M stands out as an efficient open-source engine for autonomous English narration. Derived from StyleTTS 2 principles but streamlined to 82 million parameters, its weights occupy less than 350 MB of memory. It generates 24 kHz audio across 54 built-in voice profiles covering eight languages. Because its model weights are licensed under Apache 2.0, it can be deployed within commercial production environments without licensing encumbrances. Packaged container runtimes such as kokoro-web and kokoro-onnx expose standard OpenAI-compatible /v1/audio/[span_68](start_span)[span_68](end_span)speech REST endpoints, simplifying drop-in infrastructure integration.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">CosyVoice 2.0, released by Alibaba's FunAudioLLM initiative, provides a commercially viable open-weight architecture for expressive zero-shot voice cloning. Operating under an Apache 2.0 license for both source code and pre-trained checkpoints, it utilizes a 0.5-billion-parameter flow-matching architecture capable of replicating target voices from a 3-second audio sample. It natively supports English, Chinese, Japanese, Cantonese, and Korean, and allows prosodic steering through natural language prompting. Deploying CosyVoice 2.0 requires an NVIDIA GPU with at least 8 GB of VRAM.</span></p><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">F5-TTS illustrates the risk of hidden licensing constraints in open-source AI. Although the GitHub repository is distributed under an MIT license, the pre-trained checkpoints released by the authors were trained on the large-scale Emilia speech dataset, which carries a strict Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial (CC BY-NC 4.0) license. As a result, the official base weights cannot be used within a monetized YouTube production pipeline. Using F5-TTS commercially requires training the architecture from scratch on fully permissive datasets.</span></p><h2 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Hosted APIs</span></h2><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Hosted commercial APIs offer managed scalability, high acoustic realism, and turn-key infrastructure, abstracting away the operational complexities of model serving and hardware management.</span></p><table style="border-spacing:0;border-collapse:collapse;margin-right:auto"><thead><tr style="height:0pt"><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" style="border-right-style:solid;border-bottom-color:#000000;border-top-width:1pt;border-right-w