ot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">get_model_status, list_voices, text_to_speech.</span></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" style="border-right-style:solid;border-bottom-color:#000000;border-top-width:1pt;border-right-width:1pt;border-left-color:#000000;vertical-align:top;border-right-color:#000000;border-left-width:1pt;border-top-style:solid;border-left-style:solid;border-bottom-width:1pt;width:93.6pt;border-top-color:#000000;border-bottom-style:solid"><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Immature implementation; lacks batch job queuing and sidecar timestamp returns.</span></p></td></tr></tbody></table><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Existing MCP voice implementations exhibit several limitations for automated video production. Most community MCP servers return raw audio payloads as base64-encoded strings across the stdio transport layer or save files to unmanaged local desktop directories. Transmitting large binary files over stdio consumes agent context windows and can stall JSON-RPC pipelines. Furthermore, existing servers fail to provide the structured metadata required by video compositors, omitting word alignment arrays, scene identifiers, and loudness telemetry.</span></p><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span>To resolve these bottlenecks, Hermes should not connect directly to disparate third-party MCP servers. Instead, the production engine must implement a </span><span style="font-weight:700">Unified Hermes Audio Worker Gateway</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">. This internal gateway exposes a standardized MCP tool schema to Hermes while routing jobs asynchronously to local containerized models (Kokoro-82M) or external APIs (Cartesia, ElevenLabs, Sarvam). The gateway writes generated audio directly to shared network storage and returns structured JSON responses containing file URIs, duration metrics, and word-level alignment arrays.</span></p><h2 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Licensing, Consent &amp; Commercial Use</span></h2><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Operating an automated media production business requires strict compliance with software licenses, model weight terms, publicity rights, and platform distribution policies. Conflating code licenses with model weight permissions can introduce severe legal liabilities.</span></p><table style="border-spacing:0;border-collapse:collapse;margin-right:auto"><thead><tr style="height:0pt"><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" style="border-right-style:solid;border-bottom-color:#000000;border-top-width:1pt;border-right-width:1pt;border-left-