font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Rather than designating an arbitrary "best" system, candidates are classified by their operational fit within the production engine:</span></p><ul class="lst-kix_list_2-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span>High-Volume Baseline &amp; Drafting Engine: </span><span style="font-weight:700">Kokoro-82M v1.0</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">. Its Apache 2.0 license, compact 82M parameter footprint, fast CPU execution, and absence of per-character billing make it ideal for high-throughput drafting, scratch audio, and standard English narration.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span>Low-Latency &amp; Video Synchronization Engine: </span><span style="font-weight:700">Cartesia Sonic-3.6</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">. Built on state-space models, Cartesia delivers ~40 ms first-byte response times and emits native word-level timestamps over WebSockets, making it the preferred candidate for conversational delivery, fast-paced commentary, and precise visual editing synchronization.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span>Expressive Dramatic &amp; Character Voice Engine: </span><span style="font-weight:700">ElevenLabs</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">. For cinematic documentaries, dynamic fiction, and channel-defining hero personas, ElevenLabs provides unmatched acoustic realism, organic breathing, and fine-grained voice design tools. It should be reserved for high-value hero tracks to manage operational spending.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span>Indic Multilingual &amp; Hinglish Localization Engine: </span><span style="font-weight:700">Sarvam AI (Bulbul v3)</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">. Bulbul v3 is specifically engineered for South Asian languages, delivering authentic prosody and clean, single-pass Hinglish code-switching across eleven Indian languages. It serves as the primary regional voice worker, provided inputs are formatted in native Indic scripts.</span></li></ul><h2 style="padding-top:11.2pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Targeted Validation Queue</span></h2><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">To address empirical uncertainties identified during ecosystem discovery, the following validation experiments are prioritized:</span></p><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span>| Validation Target | Core Hypothesis | Test Procedure | Evidence Required | Defined Success Criteria | Rationale for Testing | | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | | </span><span style="font-weight:700">Kokoro-82M Internal Duration Extraction</span><span>&nbsp;| Token duration matrices can be extracted from Kokoro-82M's forward pass to yield word timestamps without external WhisperX inference. | Instrument Kokoro's PyTorch duration predictor; synthesize 20 benchmark sentences; extract token boundary frames and map to words; compare against Montreal Forced Aligner output. | JSON timestamp array containing word-level start and end times in milliseconds. | Extracted timestamps match MFA boundaries within an error margin of \le 50 ms across 95% of test words. | Upstream documentation covers audio generation but does not detail how to expose internal duration tensors during inference. | | </span><span style="font-weight:700">Sarvam Bulbul v3 Hinglish Transliteration Recovery</span><span>&nbsp;| Pre-processing Romanized Hinglish through Sarvam's Transliteration API into Devanagari script eliminates phoneme distortion in Bulbul v3. | Ingest 50 tech-review sentences written in Romanized Hinglish; synthesize directly with Bulbul v3; then transliterate into Devanagari and synthesize; compare outputs via automated CER and human review. | Comparative Character Error Rate (CER) and subjective acoustic intelligibility scores. | Transliterated Devanagari input reduces pronunciation and phoneme errors by \ge 85\% compared to Romanized input. | Sarvam documents quality degradation on Romanized text but does not quantify performance recovery via automated transliteration. | | </span><span style="font-weight:700">CosyVoice 2.0 Docker Batch Serving Stability</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;| CosyVoice 2.0 can be served inside an 8 GB VRAM container to provide zero-shot character voice cloning under continuous load. | Build a containerized CosyVoice 2.0 microservice with CUDA 12.4; feed 10 distinct 5-second character audio samples; generate 100 consecutive 30-second audio files. | Continuous GPU VRAM telemetry logs, audio spectrograms, and process crash logs. | Stable VRAM allocation below 7.5 GB without memory leaks; zero audio dropouts; RTF \le 0.25. | Model weights are Apache 2.0, but operational stability during automated batch serving in memory-constrained environments is undocumented. |</span></p><h2 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Genuine Architectural Gaps</span></h2><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Discovery reveals critical architectural gaps that exist between raw speech synthesis engines and an autonomous video production system. These gaps cannot be resolved by merely switching providers; they require dedicated intermediary adapters:</span></p><ul class="lst-kix_list_3-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Dynamic Duration Fitting: Video compositing frequently requires voiceover audio to match fixed visual scene durations (e.g., an animated transition lasting exactly 4.25 seconds). Neural speech models do not accept arbitrary hard duration constraints, and forcing speed changes in diffusion models can degrade pitch.</span></li></ul><ul class="lst-kix_list_4-1 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:60pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Architectural Solution: A Hermes Duration Adapter. This component calculates the pacing delta (\Delta = T_{\text{actual}} / T_{\text{target}}) after initial synthesis. If the variation is minor (\le 12\%), it applies FFmpeg's phase-vocoder filter (atempo) to adjust audio duration without altering pitch. If the variation exceeds 12%, Hermes dynamically rewrites the script text to adjust length.</span></li></ul><ul class="lst-kix_list_3-0" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Automated Audio QA and Hallucination Auditing: Neural speech engines can occasionally hallucinate: dropping words, stuttering, repeating phrases, or introducing high-frequency squeaks when generation hyperparameters drift. In an autonomous pipeline, corrupt audio could pass directly into final video renders.</span></li></ul><ul class="lst-kix_list_5-1 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:60pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Architectural Solution: A Local Audio QA Worker. Before generated audio is accepted into the asset repository, a lightweight speech recognition pass (Faster-Whisper on CPU) transcribes the file. If the Levenshtein edit distance between the input prompt and the transcribed audio exceeds a 3% Character Error Rate, the track is automatically rejected and regenerated using an adjusted random seed.</span></li></ul><ul class="lst-kix_list_3-0" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Cross-Lingual Script and Transliteration Formatting: Large language models generating multilingual video scripts often produce mixed Latin and Indic text with inconsistent punctuation and abbreviations, which can cause synthesis errors in regional TTS engines.</span></li></ul><ul class="lst-kix_list_6-1 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:60pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Architectural Solution: A Pre-Flight Text Normalization Worker. This module expands acronyms, resolves numbers into spoken regional words, and automatically routes Indic language passages through script transliteration services prior to dispatching payloads to the voice generation engines.</span></li></ul><h2 style="padding-top:11.2pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Recommended Audio/Voice Architecture</span></h2><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The voice generation layer operates as a distributed, event-driven subsystem orchestrated by Hermes. The architecture separates script intelligence, voice selection, synthesis execution, acoustic verification, and physical mastering into modular stages.</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Orchestration and Data Flow</span></h3><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The production pipeline flows through the following discrete stages:</span></p><ol class="lst-kix_list_7-0 start" start="1" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Hermes Master Orchestrator: Issues a declarative Scene Audio Contract defining the scene identifier, narration text, character profile, language locale, target visual duration, and production priority tier (Draft, Production, or Cinematic).</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Voice Selection &amp; Routing Layer: Evaluates the contract parameters and routes the job to the appropriate engine:</span></li></ol><ul class="lst-kix_list_8-1 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:60pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Indic locales (hi-IN, ta-IN, etc.) route to the Sarvam AI Bulbul Worker.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:60pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">High-priority cinematic scenes and dramatic dialogue route to the ElevenLabs Worker.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:60pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Timing-critical commentary and dynamic video essays route to the Cartesia Sonic Worker.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:60pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Offline drafting, scratch tracks, and standard high-volume narration route to the Local Kokoro-82M Worker.</span></li></ul><ol class="lst-kix_list_7-0" start="3" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Pre-Flight Text Normalization: Expands abbreviations, standardizes numbers into spoken words, and transliterates Romanized Indic text into native scripts.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Voice Generation Worker Execution: Synthesizes the raw audio buffer at native resolution and captures word-level alignment metadata (either directly from API streams or via a secondary WhisperX alignment pass).</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Audio QA Validation Worker: Transcribes the raw audio locally using Faster-Whisper to verify text fidelity against the script contract, automatically triggering retries if errors exceed thresholds.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">FFmpeg Audio Mastering Worker: Strips leading and trailing silence, applies a 60 Hz high-pass rumble filter, resamples audio to 48 kHz 24-bit linear PCM, and performs two-pass EBU R128 loudness normalization to -14.0 LUFS.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Asset Storage &amp; Synchronization Bus: Saves the mastered WAV file and companion .words.json timing manifest to persistent network storage, emitting a completion event to Hermes to advance video compositing.</span></li></ol><h3 style="padding-top:12pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Scene Audio Contract Specification</span></h3><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The interface between Hermes and the audio subsystem is defined by a structured contract:</span></p><ul class="lst-kix_list_9-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">scene_id: Unique identifier linking the audio asset to the visual storyboard (e.g., "scene_04_breakdown").</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">text: The sanitized script to be synthesized.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">language_code: The BCP-47 language tag (e.g., "hi-IN", "en-US", "en-IN").</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">speaker_id: Target vocal identifier (e.g., a Cartesia Voice ID, an ElevenLabs Voice ID, or a Kokoro style tensor identifier).</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">emotion_intent: Target emotional styling (e.g., "documentary_authoritative", "conversational_energetic", "dramatic_whisper").</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">target_duration_ms: Target visual cut duration in milliseconds, used by downstream pacing adapters.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">priority_tier: The operational execution level ("draft", "production", "cinematic"), determining whether local open-source or commercial cloud engines are used.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">output_format: Container specification, standardizing on uncompressed 48 kHz 24-bit linear PCM WAV.</span></li></ul><h3 style="padding-top:12pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Audio Asset Store Data Schema</span></h3><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Once synthesized and mastered, voice assets are committed to shared storage using a standardized schema:</span></p><ul class="lst-kix_list_a-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Audio Master Stem: Saved as ${scene_id}_narration_master.wav (48 kHz, 24-bit mono linear PCM, normalized to -14.0 LUFS and -1.0 dBTP).</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Temporal Manifest: Saved as ${scene_id}_narration.words.json, containing an array of word objects with word string, character start and end offsets, and start and end timestamps in milliseconds.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Production Metadata: Saved as ${scene_id}_audio_meta.json, tracking the engine used, model version, random seed, integrated LUFS, True Peak, Character Error Rate from the QA pass, and generation cost.</span></li></ul><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12.8pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">This architecture decouples voice synthesis from video compositing. Hermes can execute rapid scratch-track layout passes at zero cost using local models, review scene durations, and selectively upgrade critical hero scenes to commercial APIs without reconfiguring downstream video editing workers. The division of labor preserves legal defensibility, protects operating margins, and ensures programmatic synchronization across the automated production pipeline.</span></p><h4 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:12pt;padding-bottom:12.8pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Works cited</span></h4><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">1. (PDF) F5-TTS: A Fairytaler that Fakes Fluent and Faithful Speech, https://www.researchgate.net/publication/384770900_F5-TTS_A_Fairytaler_that_Fakes_Fluent_and_Faithful_Speech_with_Flow_Matching 2. Text to Speech API - Cartesia AI, https://www.cartesia.ai/product/python-text-to-speech-api-tts 3. Voice cloning: how it works | 